# An Unoriginal Argument for a US Federal Election Day Holiday

Abigail Adams said "[The Spirit that prevails among Men of all degrees, all ages and sex'es is the Spirit of Liberty](https://www.masshist.org/publications/adams-papers/view/AFC01p204)."

The spirit of democracy is that people from all walks of life, of all backgrounds, have an equal opportunity to have their voice heard in their political system. That is the founding principal of America- "We hold these truths to be self-evident, that all men are created equal". Equality under the law and equal means to have their voice heard in government is kind of the whole point.

But that's not it is in America. There are many reasons for this, but in this article I am just going to focus on a rather simple one: the ability of different age groups to access a polling place on an election day.

## The Electorship

The driving force behind democracy is free and fair elections. Elections give the people an equal, powerful way to change government by letting them choose who in government has power. Awesome.

The US Census Bureau gives us [excellent data on the demographics](https://www.census.gov/data/tables/time-series/demo/voting-and-registration/p20-590.html) of those who voted in the 2024 elections. 

According to this beautiful data, 65.3% of all American citizens reported voting in the 2024 election. That's roughly two thirds of the entire nation, [almost average for OECD countries](https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2022/11/01/turnout-in-u-s-has-soared-in-recent-elections-but-by-some-measures-still-trails-that-of-many-other-countries/). But when you look at the voter turnout rates amongst the age groups, there is a clear difference.

18 to 24 years	27,548
25 to 34 years	38,963
35 to 44 years	38,229
45 to 54 years	35,422
55 to 64 years	37,982
65 to 74 years	33,753
75 years and over	24,242

13,150	47.7
22,148	56.8
24,340	63.7
24,339	68.7
27,015	71.1
25,389	75.2
17,928	74.0




